;;; `features/07` with its two rules in the other order: a flagged program
;;; whose answer moves with its rule order, and the engine claims nothing of
;;; a flagged program's answer.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `examples/features/07_unstratifiable.ein` with `derive-q` written before
;;; `derive-p`, and nothing else: `p ← ¬q`, `q ← ¬p`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers, and why it is wrong ───────────────────────
;;;
;;; The boundary admits candidates of the same half of the agenda in the order
;;; their rules are written (Q-M1f.313), so root derives `(q one)` here where
;;; `features/07` derives `(p one)`, the universe is `{(p one)}` where it is
;;; `{(q one)}`, and R is `{∅, {(p one)}}` where it is `{∅, {(q one)}}` (`ein
;;; solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `94c75204`, release and debug alike).
;;; The answer is not a function of the program: the config principle's *stable*
;;; (`docs/ein/reasoning/02-kb-evolution/08-monotonicity.md` § 12.1, *it does not
;;; depend … on rule order*), which today's node, a fork of root, breaks on this
;;; program and its twin.
;;;
;;;
;;; ── The right answer, by reading ───────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Which is right is what a node's state is on a flagged program, which is not
;;; decided (Q-M1e2.2 states today's assumption). Only refusing a cycle through
;;; an `absent` settles the order: this program would be refused at load. Under
;;; today's fork, R is the mirror of `features/07`'s; under a node built from
;;; scratch, both orders keep both stable models under different universes.
;;;

(relation thing Thing :why "{?1} is a thing")
(relation p Thing :why "{?1} is p")
(relation q Thing :why "{?1} is q")

(rule derive-q ()
  :match  (and (thing ?x) (absent (p ?x)))
  :assert (q ?x)
  :why    "no p for {?x}, so q")

(rule derive-p ()
  :match  (and (thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x)
  :why    "no q for {?x}, so p")

(thing one :source "the only object")
